window.docContents[111].resolve({"/Judgments-and-domains/Dynamic-judgments/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains--Dynamic-judgments":{"contents":"The judgment  states that the expression , under the environment  and the store , evaluates to  and yields . The store enters expressions because a call or a new inside an expression may change it.\n\nThe judgment  states that the expression  denotes the location . It holds for a variable, an unqualified field of this, a dereferenced pointer, a field of an object, a field through a pointer, a call of a member that returns a reference, a use of the library that gives a location, such as an element of a vector, and a conditional, which denotes the location of the chosen branch.\n\nThe judgment  states that the command  yields the control , the environment  and the store . The output environment exists so that a declaration extends  for the following commands, and the block discards the extension and frees its owned locations when it ends.\n\nThe evaluator runs in the monad , which carries the trace of the derivation. When tracing is on, each rule application records the instance of the rule it concludes, or the error, at its depth in the tree, with the antecedent and the consequent in parts. The trace records a premise of the library under the rule of its module, such as V-New, the creation of a vector in , or F-New, the creation of a function object in . The state survives the error, so the trace keeps the rule applications up to the failing rule. The log holds the instances in post order, each premise before its conclusion.The function renderTrace rebuilds the tree from the log. It lays the tree out as a derivation on the board, the premises over a line of inference, the conclusion under it and the rule name at the right.A legend names the environments , the stores  and the subjects longer than 40 characters, so every judgment fits one line. The function writes a subtree wider than 100 columns apart, under a name .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Judgments and domains","header":"2.4. Dynamic judgments","id":"/Judgments-and-domains/Dynamic-judgments/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains--Dynamic-judgments"}});